--TEST--
Core\Crypto::expandKey takes an empty salt as the zero-filled one RFC 5869 defaults to, takes an empty info as a context of its own, and answers a key under both — `rule:core-api/tier-roster`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Edges: the two arguments this derivation lets be empty, and what each empty
// one means. An empty salt is neither refused nor a special case in the member
// — HMAC pads a key shorter than its block with zeros, so extracting under no
// salt is extracting under a zero-filled one, which is the default RFC 5869
// writes for itself. That equality is the one part of the construction a
// program can see from outside, and a member that had special-cased the empty
// salt into something of its own would still answer 32 plausible octets.
secret bytes $material = Core\Crypto::generateKey();

secret bytes $noSalt = Core\Crypto::expandKey($material, "" as bytes, "encryption");
secret bytes $zeroSalt = Core\Crypto::expandKey($material, Core\Bytes::fill(32, 0), "encryption");
if ($noSalt == $zeroSalt) {
    echo "an empty salt is the zero-filled one", "\n";
}

// An empty `$info` is a context like any other rather than an absent one: it
// separates from every named context, so a program that leaves it empty at one
// call site and names it at another gets two keys and not one.
secret bytes $noInfo = Core\Crypto::expandKey($material, "" as bytes, "");
if ($noInfo != $noSalt) {
    echo "an empty info is not the context named encryption", "\n";
}

// And both edges at once still answer a key, read back the only way a
// `secret bytes` can be: by keying the ciphers that have a bound on its width.
bytes $message = "attack at dawn" as bytes;
bytes $sealed = Core\Crypto::seal($message, $noInfo, Core\Crypto\Cipher::Aes256Gcm);
if (Core\Crypto::open($sealed, $noInfo, Core\Crypto\Cipher::Aes256Gcm) == $message) {
    echo "no salt and no info: a key", "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
an empty salt is the zero-filled one
an empty info is not the context named encryption
no salt and no info: a key
